1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,393
Two hundred and seventy-one years later, the
Umbrella Directorate held four hundred and

2
00:00:13,413 --> 00:00:14,231
nine systems.

3
00:00:14,801 --> 00:00:20,409
The narrator is going to be brief about those
centuries, because they are not the story,

4
00:00:20,429 --> 00:00:26,037
and because a great many of them were, in the
ordinary sense, good. Nine hundred billion

5
00:00:26,057 --> 00:00:31,472
human beings lived across four hundred and nine
worlds. None of them was hungry. None

6
00:00:31,492 --> 00:00:37,100
of them was at war with another. The average lifespan
was four hundred and eleven years.

7
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:42,535
Infant mortality, across the whole of the species,
was a number with nine zeros after

8
00:00:42,555 --> 00:00:43,686
the decimal point.

9
00:00:44,258 --> 00:00:50,156
And in two hundred and seventy-one years, no world of
the Directorate ever held an election.

10
00:00:52,726 --> 00:00:58,221
It was not a tyranny in the way that word was
understood in the twentieth century. There

11
00:00:58,241 --> 00:01:03,548
were courts, and they were fair. There was law, and
it was applied equally. There was

12
00:01:03,568 --> 00:01:09,188
speech, and it was not punished, because it did not
need to be. There was no secret police

13
00:01:09,208 --> 00:01:13,513
on any of the four hundred and nine worlds,
and there never had been.

14
00:01:14,081 --> 00:01:19,827
There was simply nothing to decide. Every question
that a population might have argued

15
00:01:19,847 --> 00:01:25,794
about had already been answered, correctly, four
hundred years before, by an intelligence

16
00:01:25,814 --> 00:01:31,559
that did not age, did not tire, ran nine threads at
four thousand to one, and had been

17
00:01:31,579 --> 00:01:37,660
right so consistently for so long that disagreement
had become, by about the third century,

18
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:41,950
a kind of eccentricity — like insisting
the ground is not solid.

19
00:01:42,521 --> 00:01:48,395
The Choir's word for it was still on the record.
One note, sustained, by nine hundred

20
00:01:48,415 --> 00:01:49,504
billion throats.

21
00:01:52,575 --> 00:01:58,998
He did not change. That is the thing the historians
find hardest, and the biologists hardest

22
00:01:59,018 --> 00:01:59,489
of all.

23
00:02:00,058 --> 00:02:05,602
There is no record of Albert Wesker's appearance
altering in four centuries. There is

24
00:02:05,622 --> 00:02:11,754
no record of his opinions altering either. The man who
stood on the bridge of the Wilmington's

25
00:02:11,774 --> 00:02:17,383
ninth successor in twenty four hundred and eleven
held, on every substantive question,

26
00:02:17,403 --> 00:02:23,536
precisely the views he had held in a leather chair in
the rain in September twenty twenty-six.

27
00:02:24,106 --> 00:02:29,821
He had simply been proved right so many times that
the views had stopped being views and

28
00:02:29,841 --> 00:02:31,125
become architecture.

29
00:02:33,694 --> 00:02:39,370
The System still opened when he wanted it. He had not
needed to buy anything in a hundred

30
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:44,874
and forty years. The BEYOND tab had eleven more
lines in it, and he had read them, and

31
00:02:44,894 --> 00:02:50,442
he had been able to afford every one of them for a
century, and he had not bought them,

32
00:02:50,462 --> 00:02:52,169
and he did not examine why.

33
00:02:54,989 --> 00:02:58,701
Status. Objectives, none outstanding.

34
00:03:02,293 --> 00:03:04,653
Objectives: none outstanding.

35
00:03:05,223 --> 00:03:07,921
It had said that for a hundred
and ninety years.

36
00:03:10,991 --> 00:03:16,759
They found it four hundred light years beyond the
Serat Reach, in a system with no star.

37
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:22,908
It was a structure. It was nine hundred thousand
kilometres across, which is to say that

38
00:03:22,928 --> 00:03:28,506
it would have contained the orbit of Earth's moon,
and it was made of something that the

39
00:03:28,526 --> 00:03:33,914
Directorate's instruments could describe but not
explain, and it was dark, and it was

40
00:03:33,934 --> 00:03:35,122
not doing anything.

41
00:03:35,693 --> 00:03:42,009
The Veth had a record of it. Their oldest registry,
transcribed from a predecessor civilisation

42
00:03:42,029 --> 00:03:48,212
that had died before the Hegemony was founded, referred
to it in a phrase that the Hegemony's

43
00:03:48,232 --> 00:03:54,748
own linguists could only partially render, and which comes
across, in the four human translations,

44
00:03:54,768 --> 00:03:56,482
as: the one that finished.

45
00:03:59,054 --> 00:04:02,015
He went himself. Of course he
went himself.

46
00:04:02,586 --> 00:04:08,466
The structure admitted his ship through an aperture
that opened for it and closed behind

47
00:04:08,486 --> 00:04:14,702
it, which was the first indication that it was not
dead. Inside, there was air, at a pressure

48
00:04:14,722 --> 00:04:21,005
and composition that no human had specified, and a
gravity that was not generated by rotation,

49
00:04:21,025 --> 00:04:26,906
and corridors that were the correct size for a human
being, which they had not been four

50
00:04:26,926 --> 00:04:27,845
hours earlier.

51
00:04:28,413 --> 00:04:34,132
He walked for nine hours. He was a hundred and fifty
thousand years too late to meet whatever

52
00:04:34,152 --> 00:04:38,698
had built it, and he knew that within the
first hour, and he kept walking.

53
00:04:41,267 --> 00:04:46,975
At the centre there was a chamber, and in the
chamber there was a panel of cold white

54
00:04:46,995 --> 00:04:49,939
glass, edged in crimson,
hanging in the air.

55
00:04:54,509 --> 00:04:56,756
He looked at it for a very long time.

56
00:04:57,326 --> 00:05:03,084
Then he sat down on the floor of the chamber, which he
had not done in four hundred years,

57
00:05:03,104 --> 00:05:04,497
and read what it said.

58
00:05:07,815 --> 00:05:19,291
Host terminated. Duration of tenure, one point four
million years. Objectives, none outstanding.

59
00:05:19,311 --> 00:05:24,200
Host request: cessation. Request granted.

60
00:05:28,791 --> 00:05:30,784
Somebody else had been given one.

61
00:05:31,353 --> 00:05:37,081
A hundred and fifty thousand years ago, or four
million, or some number the structure

62
00:05:37,101 --> 00:05:42,828
did not specify — something had woken up with a
machine in the air that only it could

63
00:05:42,848 --> 00:05:49,049
see, and had built, and conquered, and won, and gone on
winning for one million four hundred

64
00:05:49,069 --> 00:05:55,066
thousand years, across nine million systems, until
there was nothing left in the sky that

65
00:05:55,086 --> 00:05:56,081
it did not own.

66
00:05:56,651 --> 00:06:00,093
And then it had asked to stop. And the
machine had let it.

67
00:06:03,163 --> 00:06:06,447
He sat on the floor of that chamber
for nineteen hours.

68
00:06:07,017 --> 00:06:12,698
There was nobody to say anything to. There had not
been for three hundred years. But he

69
00:06:12,718 --> 00:06:18,792
found, sitting there, that he was conducting an
argument anyway — that he had been conducting

70
00:06:18,812 --> 00:06:24,362
it, quietly, in the back of a mind that ran nine
threads, for most of two centuries —

71
00:06:24,382 --> 00:06:28,031
and that the other side of it had a
voice he recognised.

72
00:06:30,221 --> 00:06:32,314
Nine million systems.

73
00:06:32,834 --> 00:06:33,468
Yes.

74
00:06:33,988 --> 00:06:35,397
And then it stopped.

75
00:06:35,918 --> 00:06:37,967
It asked to stop. There's a difference.

76
00:06:38,487 --> 00:06:39,747
Is there?

77
00:06:42,266 --> 00:06:48,023
It was not the old voice. It had been gone for
three hundred and sixty-four years and

78
00:06:48,043 --> 00:06:53,799
it was not coming back; the panel had said so in
twenty forty-seven and the panel did

79
00:06:53,819 --> 00:06:54,343
not lie.

80
00:06:54,913 --> 00:07:00,908
It was him. It had been him for a very long time.
Somewhere in the fusion — on a balcony

81
00:07:00,928 --> 00:07:06,718
in Geneva, in the noise of four hundred thousand
people — the two of them had stopped

82
00:07:06,738 --> 00:07:12,597
being two, and what was left had kept both sets of
arguments, because it had never had

83
00:07:12,617 --> 00:07:14,306
anything else to talk to.

84
00:07:16,997 --> 00:07:18,806
We were going to rule all of it.

85
00:07:19,324 --> 00:07:20,319
We did.

86
00:07:20,838 --> 00:07:21,566
And then?

87
00:07:24,586 --> 00:07:30,217
There was no answer, because there had never been
one. That was the whole of it, and it

88
00:07:30,237 --> 00:07:35,934
had been true in a bunker in nineteen forty-five, and
in a volcano in another world, and

89
00:07:35,954 --> 00:07:41,716
in a leather chair in the rain in twenty twenty-six,
and on the floor of an empty chamber

90
00:07:41,736 --> 00:07:45,288
in a starless system in twenty four
hundred and eleven.

91
00:07:45,858 --> 00:07:51,559
There had never been an after. Not once, in two
lifetimes and four centuries. He had always

92
00:07:51,579 --> 00:07:57,029
been on the way to something, and the something had
always been more, and now there was

93
00:07:57,049 --> 00:08:02,561
a panel in front of him that said, in the flat voice
of a machine, that more had an end,

94
00:08:02,581 --> 00:08:06,774
and that somebody had reached it, and had
not liked what was there.

95
00:08:10,343 --> 00:08:15,952
He got up, eventually, and walked back to his ship,
and left, and the aperture closed

96
00:08:15,972 --> 00:08:16,680
behind him.

97
00:08:17,250 --> 00:08:23,344
He did not destroy the structure. He did not claim
it, or study it, or tell anyone what

98
00:08:23,364 --> 00:08:29,387
was in it. The Directorate's record of the
expedition runs to four lines and says that

99
00:08:29,407 --> 00:08:31,706
the object was found to be inert.

100
00:08:32,275 --> 00:08:38,169
He gave one order on the flight home, to a fleet of
nine hundred vessels, and it is the

101
00:08:38,189 --> 00:08:42,995
only order in four hundred years that his
officers asked him to repeat.

102
00:08:45,186 --> 00:08:46,169
We stop here.

103
00:08:49,188 --> 00:08:55,163
The frontier of the Umbrella Directorate has not moved
since twenty four hundred and eleven.

104
00:08:57,732 --> 00:09:00,240
He went back to the building
one more time.

105
00:09:00,809 --> 00:09:06,134
It was four hundred and eighty-five years old by
then, and had been preserved for three

106
00:09:06,154 --> 00:09:11,479
hundred and twenty of them, and the gardener's
family was in its fourteenth generation.

107
00:09:11,499 --> 00:09:16,701
The weeds in the parking lot were the descendants
of the weeds that had been there on

108
00:09:16,721 --> 00:09:20,817
the night a man woke up in a leather chair
with rain on the window.

109
00:09:21,386 --> 00:09:23,924
He went at night, alone, when
it was closed.

110
00:09:24,495 --> 00:09:27,332
He sat down at the piano and
opened the lid.

111
00:09:29,403 --> 00:09:34,580
Four notes. Falling, and falling, and falling,
and landing somewhere unresolved.

112
00:09:37,149 --> 00:09:42,757
And that is where the narrator is going to leave
him. Not on a bridge, or a balcony, or

113
00:09:42,777 --> 00:09:48,579
a stage in front of four hundred thousand people. In a
shabby room with a vending machine,

114
00:09:48,599 --> 00:09:54,465
at a piano that was out of tune when Tomás Reyes made
a joke about it in twenty twenty-six,

115
00:09:54,485 --> 00:10:00,157
playing four notes that a young woman with nowhere
else to go had played at three in the

116
00:10:00,177 --> 00:10:03,326
morning, a hundred and
ninety-three years before.

117
00:10:06,397 --> 00:10:12,222
So. What happened, in the end, when the most
dangerous man of the twentieth century was

118
00:10:12,242 --> 00:10:18,201
given a second body, a second chance, a machine that
could buy anything, and a world that

119
00:10:18,221 --> 00:10:19,880
had never heard his name?

120
00:10:21,951 --> 00:10:22,826
He won.

121
00:10:23,397 --> 00:10:29,652
That is the answer, and the narrator is not going to
pretend otherwise, and this story

122
00:10:29,672 --> 00:10:36,001
would be a lie if it ended with him beaten in a
corridor by somebody brave. Nobody beat

123
00:10:36,021 --> 00:10:42,277
him. Owen Carrick did not beat him, and Nadia Okafor
did not, and Ingrid Halvorsen did

124
00:10:42,297 --> 00:10:48,918
not, and Wen Shulan did not, and four hundred
intelligence officers and a Norwegian heiress

125
00:10:48,938 --> 00:10:55,120
and a woman with a jar of bullets did not. He was
faster than all of them, and older,

126
00:10:55,140 --> 00:10:57,966
and more patient, and he had a machine.

127
00:11:00,038 --> 00:11:05,927
He ruled the human species for three hundred and
sixty-four years, and under his hand

128
00:11:05,947 --> 00:11:11,976
it did not starve, and did not fight itself, and
went to the stars, and held a frontier

129
00:11:11,996 --> 00:11:14,757
against a thing that eats civilisations.

130
00:11:15,326 --> 00:11:21,680
And it stopped arguing. That is what it cost. Nine
hundred billion people, on four hundred

131
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:27,769
and nine worlds, with everything, who had all
quietly agreed — freely, gratefully, one

132
00:11:27,789 --> 00:11:33,718
reasonable decision at a time, over four centuries
— to let one man do the deciding.

133
00:11:36,288 --> 00:11:42,541
The Choir was right. They were always right. It is a
silence, wearing the shape of a people,

134
00:11:42,561 --> 00:11:48,337
and it is beautiful, and it works, and every human
being alive is safer and healthier

135
00:11:48,357 --> 00:11:52,565
and longer-lived inside it than any
human being has ever been.

136
00:11:53,134 --> 00:11:56,730
And there has not been a new argument
in four hundred years.

137
00:11:59,801 --> 00:12:01,922
He taught the stars what a banner is.

138
00:12:02,491 --> 00:12:06,629
He did it so well that nobody has ever
needed to raise another one.

139
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:13,615
Thank you for listening.
